<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#chatusadom">Glossary</a> · Term · government</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chatusadom</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1454">1454</a>–<a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Catustambha, Four Pillars, Wiang-Wang-Khlang-Na</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chatusadom, the Four Pillars, was the Thai system of central government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, Thonburi and Rattanakosin kingdoms from 1454 to 1892. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommatrailokkanat">Trailokanat</a> set it out in his Palatine Law of 1454, organising four existing ministries under ministers of the rank of Phraya, with two prime ministers, the Samuhanayok for civil and the Samuhakalahom for military affairs, ruling on the king's behalf. Over time the line between civil and military officials blurred, and the Samuhakalahom grew so powerful that one of them seized the throne as King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasat-thong">Prasat Thong</a> in 1629. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a> reorganised the system into modern ministries and established the Cabinet in 1892.</p></div>
